<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramachandra (Yadava dynasty)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1311</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ramachandra of Devagiri, Ramadeva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramachandra was the last major king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a>, ruling from Devagiri from 1271. In 1296 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> raided Devagiri and forced him to pay a huge tribute. After a renewed invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> in 1308 he became a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and soon after his death the kingdom was annexed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramachandra-of-devagiri.jpg" alt="Ramachandra (Yadava dynasty)" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seuna_(Yadava)._Ramachandra._1270-1311_Central_lotus_blossom,_two_%C5%9Bri,_conch,_and_%E2%80%9C%C5%9Bri_rama%E2%80%9D_in_Devanagari_above_standard_left,_each_in_incuse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramachandra was a son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. He was probably very young when his father died around 1260, so the throne passed to his uncle Mahadeva and then, around 1270, to Mahadeva's son Ammana. Many leading officers regarded Ramachandra as the rightful heir, and the courtiers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemadri">Hemadri</a> and Tikkama deserted Ammana for him. In the second half of 1271 Ramachandra and his followers entered the fort of Devagiri disguised as actors and seized Ammana during a performance. Mahanubhava texts say he blinded his cousin; the Nagadeva-Charita says he killed him, a claim historians doubt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He fought all his neighbours. He invaded the weakened Paramara kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in the 1270s, and both he and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a> of Gurjara claimed victory in skirmishes between them. In the autumn of 1275 his general Saluva Tikkama invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> kingdom and besieged its capital Dvarasamudra, until a Hoysala attack on 25 April 1276 forced him to retreat with large plunder. Against the Kakatiyas he backed chiefs opposed to Queen Rudrama, who answered by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> territory. His Purushottamapuri inscription claims conquests in the north-east as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, where he is said to have built a temple to Sharangadhara; A. S. Altekar accepted an occupation of the city, while P. M. Joshi called the claims hollow.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s raid of 1296 caught him with much of his army away under the crown prince Simhana. Ramachandra agreed to pay tribute, and when Simhana returned and was defeated a heavier one was imposed. The Kakatiyas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Ballala III then seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> lands. After he stopped paying in 1303-1304, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> defeated the Yadava army and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, where Alauddin received him courteously, restored him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, gave him the title Raja-i-Rajan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> of Navsari. According to the chronicler Isami, Ramachandra also gave his daughter, called Jhatyapali or Chhitai, to Alauddin in marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ramachandra helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> against the Kakatiyas in 1309 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> in 1311. His last inscription is dated 1311, and he probably died that year. His son Simhana III rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> and was defeated and killed, and another son, Bhima, set up a base at Mahikavati, modern Mahim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>. Yadava records call Ramachandra a great devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemadri">Hemadpant</a> is credited with five temples at Ramtek.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramachandra of Devagiri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court supported Sanskrit and early Marathi literature: the astronomer Bhaskara's grandson ran a school under Seuna patronage, and Hemadri, minister to Mahadeva and <b>Ramachandra</b>, wrote the Chaturvarga Chintamani and is credited with the Hemadpanti temple style.</blockquote><a href="/state/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
